{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ וגו׳.</b> It is difficult: why was it necessary to say <b>וַיֵּלְכוּ</b> after He has already made us hear the going? And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 35a) that it was written to juxtapose the going to the coming — just as the coming was with an evil counsel, etc. And we need to know for what matter the verse is making us hear this thing, that they went with an evil counsel?" "<b>Now</b>, the matter will be explained based on what they said in tractate Kiddushin folio 39a, and this is their language: It was taught, Rabbi Yaakov says: there is no mitzvah, etc. — suppose his father said to him: go up to the roof and bring me fledglings, and he went up and took the fledglings and sent the mother away, and on his return he fell and died — where is the lengthening of this one's days? Rather, in any case all is for the good, etc. And the Gemara raised the difficulty that Rabbi Yaakov saw an actual event, and asked: perhaps he was having thoughts of idol worship? And they answered: he too said the same thing — if you suppose that the reward of a mitzvah exists in this world, why did the mitzvah not protect him so that he would not come to such thoughts? End of quote. This implies that according to the one who says that the reward of a mitzvah exists in this world, the mitzvah protects from suffering and from sin. And it is difficult, for in tractate Sotah folio 21a, at the conclusion of the matter according to Rava, it says: a mitzvah protects and saves, but from sin in the time itself that one is engaged in it, it does not save. If so, by what does Rabbi Yaakov prove from the fact that the mitzvah did not protect the one going up the ladder from coming to thoughts of idol worship? And moreover, according to the reasoning of Rav Yosef that the Gemara brings in Sotah — that a mitzvah at the time one is engaged in it protects him also from transgressions — is not the reasoning of Rav Yosef rejected because of the difficulty of Doeg and Ahitofel, whose Torah did not protect them from going out to a bad culture? And since the words of Rav Yosef were rejected, by what does the Gemara in Kiddushin justify the difficulty of Rabbi Yaakov?" "<b>And there are those</b> who wished to distinguish between the act of a mitzvah and the act of a mitzvah: what they said in Sotah, that a mitzvah does not save from sin even at the time one is engaged in it, speaks of one who acts not for its own sake; and there, where they asked why it did not protect them from coming to sin, speaks of a mitzvah for its own sake — end of their words. And this is nothing, for if so the Talmud should have rejected the event of the ladder on the grounds that he did not perform the mitzvah for its own sake, which is a more proximate rejection than the rejection of ״perhaps he was having thoughts of idol worship.״ Moreover, with this rejection he would have been able to avoid the question of why the mitzvah did not protect him from the evil inclination, since according to this there is no power in that mitzvah to protect from the evil inclination." "<b>And the correct</b> resolution is this: the conclusion of the Talmud follows the reasoning of Rav Yosef, that a mitzvah at the time one is engaged in it protects and saves, and therefore Rabbi Yaakov proves from the fact that the mitzvah did not protect and save the one going up the ladder to bring fledglings to his father — for behold, even on the return it was the time of performing a mitzvah, since he was bringing fledglings to his father. And what the Gemara in Sotah rejected — the reasoning of Rav Yosef from the fact that Torah did not protect Doeg and Ahitofel — is not a rejection, according to what they said in Chagigah (Chagigah 15a): in the Mishnah it was taught: Doeg and Ahitofel had filth in their hearts, meaning that their Torah was not for its own sake; and from this there is no proof from the fact that their Torah did not save them, for it was not Torah for its own sake. Or according to what the Tosafot explained there, that they were people of transgression before they engaged in Torah and their Torah preceded their fear of sin — in such a case Torah does not save them from the evil inclination." "<b>Furthermore,</b> even according to the one who rejects the reasoning of Rav Yosef in tractate Sotah on account of Doeg and Ahitofel, he rejected only the attribution of the Torah's benefit that it does not save (even) when one is not engaged in it; but what he said — that a mitzvah at the time one is engaged in it protects and saves — the Gemara did not reject. And even though Rava, because of the difficulty of Doeg and Ahitofel, was compelled to interpret the beraita that said that the verse suspended the mitzvah in ״a lamp״ and the Torah in ״light,״ etc. — let him say that there is no benefit in a mitzvah except to protect from troubles, but there is no necessity to say so according to him. And one can say on this path: a mitzvah at the time one is engaged in it protects and saves, and at the time one is not engaged in it — nothing. Torah at the time one is engaged in it protects and saves, and at the time one is not engaged in it protects but does not save. And what they said, ״if she has merit it suspends״ — this refers to the merit of Torah, which saves from punishment even not at the time one is engaged in it; and even though a woman is not commanded regarding Torah, it is through the reward of the Torah of their husbands and their sons, and according to the words of Ravina there (Sotah 21a). And on this path Rabbi Yaakov proves from the one going up the ladder to bring fledglings to his father, that his mitzvah did not save him at the time he was engaged in it." "<b>And further,</b> when we plumb the matter deeply we must set out the framework of the Talmud's discussion in Kiddushin even according to Rava's position, by raising what the Gemara said — perhaps he was entertaining thoughts of idolatry. This is difficult for him: if he was entertaining thoughts of idolatry, would the mitzvah not have saved him from suffering, as is evident from the passage of the sotah, where they said if she has merit it suspends [her punishment]? And there in the case of the sotah she had certainly sinned, yet he [Rava] says that merit prevents her suffering even according to Rava's position; and there her merit is remote, whereas in our case, where he went to bring back stolen property, his merit stands right before him. And to resolve this, one is compelled to say that insofar as he was entertaining thoughts of idolatry, he is like one who denies the entire Torah, and he is like a gentile in all his affairs, and he has no mitzvah to protect him. Or it is possible that insofar as at the time of performing the mitzvah he was entertaining thoughts of sin, there is no benefit in the mitzvah he performs to protect him at all. And he [further] resolves that according to R. Yaakov's reasoning, if we say that the reward of a mitzvah in this world would have protected him from encountering anything that brings punishment in its wake — for if you do not say thus, then it does not protect him even from the punishment either. And also according to Rava's reasoning, who said that a mitzvah while it is being performed protects from punishment, this necessarily includes that he will not come to commit a sin that brings the punishment in its wake — for without this, even from the punishment itself he is not guaranteed [protection]." "We thus find ourselves saying that according to R. Yosef's reasoning, a mitzvah at the time of action saves from the evil inclination; and also according to Rava's reasoning, in the second resolution, if the sin would bring punishments in this world, the mitzvah protects him from it. Therefore the verse here comes to resolve the difficulty that is felt: why did the mitzvah not protect them, since they went on a mission of mitzvah, as it is written ״וַיִּשְׁלַח אוֹתָם מֹשֶׁה עַל פִּי יהוה״ — to save them from the punishments that would come upon them? And it says <b>וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ</b>, meaning to juxtapose their going with their coming: just as their coming was with an evil plan, so too when they went it was with an evil plan. If so, there is no mitzvah here at all, and on the contrary, they are emissaries of transgression." "<b>Furthermore,</b> the reason the verse needed to make this known to us appears to be based on what they said there in Kiddushin (Kiddushin 39b), after resolving according to R. Yaakov's reasoning that [the mitzvah] should have protected him from coming to sin — the Talmud returned to ask: but did not R. Elazar say that emissaries of a mitzvah are not harmed? And it answers: the ladder was rickety — end of quotation. And it is difficult: what difficulty is this, after the Gemara said that he was entertaining thoughts of idolatry? Why should this not also resolve the difficulty of ״emissaries of a mitzvah are not harmed״? Rather, it is certainly the case that emissaries of a mitzvah do derive benefit from the mission of the mitzvah, namely that no evil effect will be drawn upon them from the mission itself — and it is implied from the Gemara that this is an agreed-upon matter. From this it follows that the verse was obliged to say <b>וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ</b>, to uproot from them the designation of a mitzvah-mission — for they went only with an evil plan, and therefore they were harmed in their mission." "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, the statement <b>וַיֵּלְכוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ</b> may be explained in the manner that the masters of the sod, of blessed memory, spoke (Sotah 3a): when a great person has an opportunity for sin before him and draws near to commit it, his exalted soul departs from him and a spirit of folly enters him, and then he draws near to do it. And insofar as the verses testify that those men were princes of Israel and great of soul, therefore the verse testifies that at the time of the evil deed, when they came to Moses to speak words of wickedness, they ״went״ — meaning the holy lights departed from their souls — and then ״they came״ [וַיָּבֹאוּ]." "<b>Furthermore,</b> it alludes in the statement <b>וַיֵּלְכוּ</b> that they made a decisive departure from the land of Canaan, never to return to it — as they revealed through the words they spoke to the people. The Torah too rendered a final verdict upon them: that their going was an eternal departure, and they would not return to see it again. And its saying <b>וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל מֹשֶׁה</b> etc. comes to give a reason why they came and were not lost on their mission: <b>אֶל מֹשֶׁה</b> etc. — meaning, HaShem was mindful of the honor of Moses, and therefore they completed the mission and came. Furthermore, it was so that Moses and Aaron and the congregation would see the deed of HaShem — for a few men went up among many great and mighty nations and escaped — and from this they would justify that likewise HaShem would do with them, to cast His terror upon all the peoples. ״וִישָׁרִים דַּרְכֵי יהוה״ etc. (Hosea 14:10)." "<b>To the wilderness of Paran, Kadesh.</b> One needs to know why Scripture mentions ״Kadeshah״ only on the return and not on the going. Perhaps it alluded in the word <b>Kadeshah</b> to that which they were delayed at Kadesh because of the spies, as it is written (Deuteronomy 1:46): ״וַתֵּשְׁבוּ בְקָדֵשׁ יָמִים רַבִּים כַּיָּמִים אֲשֶׁר יְשַׁבְתֶּם״, and they of blessed memory said (Rashi there) nineteen years they spent in Kadesh and nineteen years in the return journey from Kadesh until they reached it a second time — behold, thirty-eight years. From this, at Kadeshah and in its circuiting they were delayed all the time that the spies caused them to be delayed in the wilderness, and this is the verse's meaning: ״וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל מֹשֶׁה וְגוֹ׳ אֶל כָּל עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל קָדֵשָׁה״." "<b>And they answered them a matter.</b> Scripture concealed their evil answer and said <b>a matter</b> without specification, for it is not the way of Scripture to write the deeds of the wicked except out of necessity — as you will find that it did not write the killing of Hur on the day of the making of the calf, and likewise the making of the calf — they said in the Gemara (Avodah Zarah 4a) that it was written only to teach Israel that if the public sinned, one says to them: go to the public. And similarly in what is before us, it concealed the matters until it became necessary to say that Israel wanted to return to Egypt and rebelled against HaShem, Heaven forbid; then it was necessary to state the cause of the matter and to reveal the thing that they put out an evil report of the land. And the reason Scripture needed to state the evil report of Israel is to make known the reason for their delay in the wilderness forty years, which was because of their evil report." "And it says <b>and all the congregation</b> — meaning, they answered him when the whole congregation was present, and they did not single him out to speak evil things to him in private, for they intended to disgrace him in the eyes of all Israel:" "<b>Or</b> it may intend in this manner: <b>and they answered them a matter</b> — that which is stated immediately above, and Scripture comes to make known that they paid honor to Moses to single him out for the answer according to royal law, and afterward they answered also the whole congregation." "<b>Or</b> it may intend according to what they of blessed memory said regarding the statement that we wrote in the first verse, that Moses was caught by their hands through the argument of ״וְיַחְפְּרוּ לָנוּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״ and so forth until here. Say then that the reason for the sending of Israel was a reason unto itself, and the reason for the sending of Moses was a reason unto itself. Therefore Scripture said <b>and they answered them a matter</b> — to Moses and Aaron regarding the matters that he said to them in his sending; <b>and all the congregation</b> — regarding the matters that they said to them in their sending."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {5 "The comment engages with the Talmudic discussion in Kiddushin 39b–40a regarding whether reward for a mitzvah is paid in this world or the next, and the principle that mitzvah-performance at the time of action shields from harm. 'סוטה' refers to the Talmudic passage in Sotah dealing with merit suspending punishment. The term 'אגוני' is Aramaic for 'while suffering' or 'in the midst of distress,' here meaning during the act of performance."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:27.246622511Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}